From: Aspartame GTK4 migration
Subject: [PATCH] home: restore the top bar clock and the universal help icon

The classic Space's Home toolbar carries a centred clock and the universal
help icon. Neither was ever ported, so the modern Space's top bar was
empty between the search entry and the view buttons.

Clock. Built in the toolbar but placed by HomeWindow as an overlay child,
which is what the GTK3 shell does: keeping it out of the toolbar's
allocation flow stops a changing search entry or view button count from
shifting the time sideways. It takes the toolbar band's height and lets
the label centre its own text, rather than being offset by its natural
height - that centres the line box and leaves the digits sitting high.
Measured in the 75px band, the glyphs land at rows 25..41 against a band
centre of 37, matching the classic Space's placement.

Help icon. Sugar SVGs colour themselves through internal DTD entities
(&stroke_color;, &fill_color;), and the GdkPixbuf loader behind
Gtk.Image.new_from_file() does not expand them: the icon rasterises
completely transparent. Rendering this file through Gtk.Image produced a
200x200 texture whose PNG is 263 bytes - an empty image - against 2366
bytes once the entities are substituted by hand. Sugar's own Icon does
that substitution and hands the result to librsvg, so use it.

The classic shell turns this icon into Select-a-Thing, which has no GTK4
port. Until it does, the button opens the Help activity - the same thing
the icon points at - rather than leaving the control out of the toolbar.

--- a/src/jarabe/desktop/viewtoolbar.py
+++ b/src/jarabe/desktop/viewtoolbar.py
